kind: fix
upstream: not yet reported to draco.

Two bugs in EncodeSymbols, the second only reachable once the first is fixed,
so they go together.

1. It estimates what the raw scheme would cost before it checks that the raw
scheme can encode these values at all. The estimate is ComputeShannonEntropy,
which allocates one int for every value up to the largest symbol, not one per
symbol, and takes that largest symbol as an int. Anything over
kMaxRawEncodingBitLength (18 bits) is always tagged, so all of that work
decides nothing. Now the estimate is only made where it can matter: the method
chosen doesn't change, and the table is at most 1 MiB.

What it cost depends on the largest symbol, at any encoding setting, on any
machine. Measured 2026-11-07 on the manylinux x86_64 wheel of commit 043afef,
through trimkit.exchange.draco_encode.

float32 POSITION, 500 vertices, quantization_bits as listed:
  bits 18:      0.001 s,    35 MiB peak
  bits 22:      0.008 s,    50 MiB
  bits 26:      0.117 s,   289 MiB
  bits 30:      1.645 s,  4129 MiB, and MemoryError in a 32-bit process

one more GENERIC attribute, 498 vertices, default settings:
  uint32 up to 2**28 - 1:   1051 MiB
  uint32 up to 2**31 - 1:   8171 MiB, 3.6 s
  int32, the full range:    std::length_error, max_value is negative
  int32 holding -2**31:     SIGSEGV, 6 vertices are enough

The last one zigzags to the symbol 0xFFFFFFFF, which as an int is -1, so
max_value + 1 is 0, the vector is empty and ++symbol_frequencies[symbol]
writes through null. Reproduce it with:
  faces = np.arange(6, dtype=np.int64).reshape((-1, 3))
  position = np.random.default_rng(1).random((6, 3)).astype(np.float32)
  value = np.array([0, -2**31] * 3, np.int32)
  kinds = np.array([DracoKind.POSITION, DracoKind.GENERIC], np.int64)
  draco_encode(faces, [position, value], kinds)

2. The tagged scheme counts bit lengths 1 to 32 in an array of 32, so a symbol
of 2**31 or more writes frequencies[32], past the end of the stack array, and
then encodes a tag its table doesn't hold. Every such symbol crashed in 1
first, which is the only reason this didn't. One more entry fixes both.
RAnsSymbolEncoder::Create drops trailing empty entries, so every other buffer
is byte for byte the same, and the decoder reads a table of any size and bit
lengths up to 32, so an unpatched draco reads these buffers too.

Not fixed here: past a range of 2**31 - 2 the wrap transform's bounds fail, and
PredictionSchemeWrapEncodingTransform::Init ignores that, so draco writes a
buffer its own decoder refuses. That is its format's limit, so src/draco.cpp
refuses those integers before encoding, including every case in 1 that crashed.

Why nobody hits these: draco's usual settings put positions at 11 to 14 bits and
everything else lower, so symbols stay far below the 18 bits where this starts.
glTF's attribute types are 8 and 16 bit integers and floats.

Covered by: tests/test_draco.py and fuzz/draco_roundtrip.cpp.

--- a/src/draco/compression/entropy/symbol_encoding.cc
+++ b/src/draco/compression/entropy/symbol_encoding.cc
@@ -28,7 +28,8 @@
 constexpr int kMaxRawEncodingBitLength = 18;
 constexpr int kDefaultSymbolCodingCompressionLevel = 7;
 
-typedef uint64_t TaggedBitLengthFrequencies[kMaxTagSymbolBitLength];
+// One per bit length, up to and including kMaxTagSymbolBitLength.
+typedef uint64_t TaggedBitLengthFrequencies[kMaxTagSymbolBitLength + 1];
 
 void SetSymbolEncodingMethod(Options* options, SymbolCodingMethod method) {
   options->SetInt("symbol_encoding_method", method);
@@ -134,17 +135,21 @@
   const int64_t tagged_scheme_total_bits =
       ApproximateTaggedSchemeBits(bit_lengths, num_components);
 
-  // Approximate number of bits needed for storing the symbols using the raw
-  // scheme.
-  int num_unique_symbols = 0;
-  const int64_t raw_scheme_total_bits = ApproximateRawSchemeBits(
-      symbols, num_values, max_value, &num_unique_symbols);
-
   // The maximum bit length of a single entry value that we can encode using
   // the raw scheme.
   const int max_value_bit_length =
       MostSignificantBit(std::max(1u, max_value)) + 1;
 
+  // Approximate number of bits needed for storing the symbols using the raw
+  // scheme, which counts every value up to max_value: only for values the raw
+  // scheme can encode, as anything longer is tagged whatever the estimate.
+  int num_unique_symbols = 0;
+  int64_t raw_scheme_total_bits = 0;
+  if (max_value_bit_length <= kMaxRawEncodingBitLength) {
+    raw_scheme_total_bits = ApproximateRawSchemeBits(
+        symbols, num_values, max_value, &num_unique_symbols);
+  }
+
   int method = -1;
   if (options != nullptr && options->IsOptionSet("symbol_encoding_method")) {
     method = options->GetInt("symbol_encoding_method");
@@ -201,7 +206,7 @@
 
   // Create encoder for encoding the bit tags.
   SymbolEncoderT<5> tag_encoder;
-  tag_encoder.Create(frequencies, kMaxTagSymbolBitLength, target_buffer);
+  tag_encoder.Create(frequencies, kMaxTagSymbolBitLength + 1, target_buffer);
 
   // Start encoding bit tags.
   tag_encoder.StartEncoding(target_buffer);
